------------------------------------------------
PART 3 - QUESTION 3: MERGE SORT IMPLEMENTATION
------------------------------------------------

(a) How GenAI merge sort code was checked for correctness:

The merge sort algorithm generated by AI was verified for correctness through several validation steps:

1. Code Review: The implementation was thoroughly reviewed to ensure it followed the standard merge sort algorithm with the three key steps:
   - Dividing the list into two halves (using the slow/fast pointer technique to find the middle)
   - Recursively sorting each half
   - Merging the sorted halves back together

2. Test Cases: The implementation was tested with various input lists including:
   - Empty list and single-node list (edge cases)
   - Already sorted list (best case)
   - Reverse-sorted list (worst case)
   - List with duplicate values
   - The specific test case provided in the assignment [35, 62, 15, 24, 40, 7]

3. Output Verification: For each test case, I verified that:
   - The output was correctly sorted in ascending order
   - The structure of the linked list remained intact
   - No nodes were lost during the sorting process
   - The algorithm worked with the input data types (integers)

4. Time/Space Complexity Analysis: I confirmed the implementation maintained the expected O(n log n) time complexity characteristic of merge sort.

5. Direct Execution: The code was compiled and executed independently in mergeSortGenAI.cpp to confirm its standalone functionality before integration.

(b) Changes made to adapt code to LinkedBag:

Adapting the merge sort algorithm from mergeSortGenAI.cpp to the LinkedBag class required several modifications:

1. Interface Adaptation:
   - Changed the method signatures to match LinkedBag's design
   - Added helper methods to the LinkedBag class (mergeSortHelper, findMiddle, merge)
   - Modified the sort() method to call mergeSort() when method=0

2. Data Structure Differences:
   - Updated code to work with Node<ItemType> instead of ListNode
   - Modified accessor methods to use getItem()/setItem() instead of direct val access
   - Changed pointer navigation to use getNext()/setNext() instead of direct next access

3. Memory Management:
   - Added proper memory management for the dummy node in the merge function
   - Ensured no memory leaks during the sorting process

4. Type Handling:
   - Modified the code to work with generic types using templates
   - Replaced explicit int comparisons with generic comparisons for ItemType

5. Integration with Existing Code:
   - Preserved the existing LinkedBag interface
   - Maintained headPtr as the class member instead of returning a new head
   - Ensured compatibility with all existing LinkedBag methods

6. Error Handling:
   - Added checks for empty bags and single-element bags
   - Made sure proper null checks were in place throughout the algorithm

These adaptations ensured the merge sort algorithm worked correctly within the LinkedBag class structure while maintaining its efficiency and correctness.